    {
        "content": "<p>The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P-variation\">p-variation</a> of a function from an ordered set to a metric space is a way to vary the regularity/smoothness of a function and a direct generalisation of the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Total_variation\">total variation</a> of a function (which we do have in <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Analysis/BoundedVariation.html\">mathlib</a>).</p>\n<p>The p-variation (also called r-variation) of a function is used quite widely in for example harmonic analysis and (apparently) probability theory and seems like a good addition to mathlib, especially considering the fact we have the special case p=1 already. It is also closely related to Hölder functions in various ways.</p>\n<p>However there are two questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>How should the p-variation be defined?<br>\nNamely, one can easily generalise the definition of bounded variation by adding an exponent (I used this definition in the PR <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37007\">#37007</a>, which has to be fixed).<br>\nHowever one may define the p-variation more discretely using L^p norms:<br>\nFor f:A\\to B an ordered tuple S=(a_0,...,a_n) define f_S:{1,...,n}\\to B as f(k)=d(f(k),f(k-1)). The the p-variation of f is equal to the supremum of the L^p norms over all such S.<br>\nThis discrete definition extends more naturally to the edge cases p=\\infty and (less importantly) p=0, however one does not gain any significant insight whatsoever using the L^p norm definition. (It is however easy to get from the L^p definition again back to the original definition in Lean)<br>\nSo what would be the more natural way of defining the p-variation? I'm leaning towards the \"direct\" definition.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What to do with the definition of total variation (i.e. case p=1) we already have?<br>\nThe p-variation is a very easy generalisation. So either change the definition of total variation we have to be \"p-variation where p=1\" or keep it seperate (similar like Nat.choose is a special case of Ring.choose but not defined in terms of Ring.choose).</p>\n</li>\n</ol>",
        "id": 583883733,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1775517539
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'd go for the direct definition, because the case p=infinity is not interesting and it has a serious cost (if the exponent is ENNReal-valued, then you will need to insert cast in many places, and the definition won't be as nice to use). For the case <code>p = 1</code>, I would express it in terms of the general definition, to avoid code duplication in proofs.</p>",
        "id": 583916852,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775541114
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"287929\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Formalising.20Young.20integral.2C.20policy.20on.20code.20duplication/with/578389056\">#mathlib4 &gt; Formalising Young integral, policy on code duplication</a> Maybe <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"346307\">@Emilio Ferrucci</span> have already done sth about p-variation?</p>",
        "id": 584043448,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1775581149
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi, thanks for the mention <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"669043\">@Yongxi Lin (Aaron)</span>.</p>\n<p>I did work a little on this, mostly as an exercise for myself to learn lean, but it's not PR-ready so happy for someone else to have a go. However I'm also happy to share my file (which compiles) if you want to have a look.</p>\n<p>Some comments (as a non-lean expert):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>I found it a bit difficult to avoid conceptual duplication between the new notions of p-variation and what's already in the file Topology/EMetricSpace/BoundedVariation.lean. I wrote my definitions in a separate file and then proved a little lemma that says that \"PVariationOn =  eVariationOn for p = 1\", but to do things properly I think one should work in the same file and go through the proofs one by one and extract parts that are in common.</li>\n<li>For example, p-variation (to the p-th power) is superadditive on disjoint intervals, but not additive. </li>\n<li>I think the definition that is most used is, as you say, just add an exponent. The range that is interesting for the applications I had in mind is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p \\in [1,\\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>.</li>\n<li>I also proved lemmas for: comparison between p- and q-var, and continuity in the sense that for sequences <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_n \\leq t_n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.786em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7651em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_n - s_n \\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7651em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5806em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>, for $f$ continuous <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><mi>f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\| f \\|_{p-var, [s_n, t_n]} \\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1052em;vertical-align:-0.3552em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∥</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">∥</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5198em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span><span class=\"mbin mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">v</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">a</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">r</span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mopen mtight\">[</span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">s</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1645em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.357em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.143em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">t</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1645em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.357em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.143em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose mtight\">]</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3552em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span> (this is needed for Young integration).</li>\n<li>The relationship with Hölder functions I know of is that a continuous function is of bounded p-variation iff it is reparametrisable as a 1/p-Hölder function, but I think this could be a bit tricky to show in lean</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 584052029,
        "sender_full_name": "Emilio Ferrucci",
        "timestamp": 1775583841
    },
